A necessary and sufficient condition that the boundary value problem:
\begin{align*}
  \frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial x^2} + \frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial y^2} &= f(x, y) \quad \text{in } \Omega \\
  \frac{\partial u}{\partial n} &= 0 \quad \text{on } \partial\Omega
\end{align*}
(where $\Omega \subset \R^2$ is a bounded domain with boundary $\partial\Omega$ and $\frac{\partial u}{\partial n}$ denotes the outer normal derivative of the function $u$) has a solution is \underline{\hspace{2cm}}.

Which two of the following spaces are homeomorphic to each other?
\begin{enumerate}[i)]
  \item $X_1 = \{(x, y) \in \R^2 : xy = 0\}$;
  \item $X_2 = \{(x, y) \in \R^2 : x + y \ge 0 \text{ and } xy = 0\}$;
  \item $X_3 = \{(x, y) \in \R^2 : xy = 1\}$;
  \item $X_4 = \{(x, y) \in \R^2 : x + y \ge 0 \text{ and } xy = 1\}$.
\end{enumerate}

Which of the following spaces are compact?
\begin{enumerate}[i)]
  \item $X_1 = \{(x, y) \in \R^2 : |x| + |y| < 10^{-100}\}$;
  \item $X_2 = \{(x, y) \in \R^2 : |x| + |y| \le 10^{100}\}$;
  \item $X_3 = \{(x, y) \in \R^2 : 1 \le x^2 + y^2 \le 2\}$;
  \item $X_4 = \{(x, y) \in \R^2 : x^2 + y^2 = 1 \text{ and } xy \neq 0\}$.
\end{enumerate}

Which of the following spaces are locally compact?
\begin{enumerate}[i)]
  \item $X_1 = \{(x, y) \in \R^2 : x, y \text{ odd integers}\}$;
  \item $X_2 = \{(x, y) \in \R^2 : x^2 + 103xy + 7y^2 > 5\}$;
  \item $X_3 = \{(x, y) \in \R^2 : 0 \le x < 1,\ 0 < y \le 1\}$;
  \item $X_4 = \{(x, y) \in \R^2 : x, y \text{ irrational}\}$.
\end{enumerate}

On which of the following spaces is every continuous (real-valued) function bounded?
\begin{enumerate}[i)]
  \item $X_1 = ]0, 1[$;
  \item $X_2 = [0, 1]$;
  \item $X_3 = [0, 1[$;
  \item $X_4 = \{t \in [0, 1] : t \text{ irrational}\}$.
\end{enumerate}

Let $\Delta = \partial^2/\partial x^2 + \partial^2/\partial y^2$ denote the Laplace operator. Let $\Omega = \{(x, y) \in \R^2 : x^2 + y^2 < 1\}$. Let $\partial\Omega$ denote the boundary of the domain $\Omega$. Consider the following boundary value problem:
\begin{align*}
  \Delta u &= c \quad \text{in } \Omega \\
  \frac{\partial u}{\partial \nu} &= 1 \quad \text{on } \partial\Omega
\end{align*}
where $c$ is a real constant and $\partial u/\partial \nu$ denotes the outward normal derivative of $u$ on $\partial\Omega$. For what values of $c$ does the above problem admit a solution?

Determine the value of the least possible positive number $\lambda$ such that the following problem has a non-trivial solution:
\begin{align*}
  u''(x) + \lambda u(x) &= 0, \quad 0 < x < 1 \\
  u'(0) = u'(1) &= 0.
\end{align*}

Find d'Alembert's solution to the problem:
\begin{align*}
  \frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial t^2} &= \frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial x^2}, \quad x \in \R,\ t > 0 \\
  u(x, 0) &= x^2, \quad x \in \R \\
  \frac{\partial u}{\partial t}(x, 0) &= 0, \quad x \in \R.
\end{align*}

What is the necessary and sufficient condition for the following problem to admit a solution?
\begin{align*}
  -\Delta u &= f \quad \text{in } \Omega \\
  \frac{\partial u}{\partial n} &= g \quad \text{on } \partial\Omega
\end{align*}
where $\Omega \subset \R^n$ is a bounded domain with boundary $\partial\Omega$, $\Delta$ is the Laplace operator, $f$ and $g$ are given smooth functions and $\frac{\partial u}{\partial n}$ denotes the outer normal derivative of $u$.

Let $p$ be a prime and consider the field $\Z_p$. List the primes for which the following system of linear equations DOES NOT have a solution in $\Z_p$:
\begin{align*}
  5x + 3y &= 4 \\
  3x + 6y &= 1.
\end{align*}

Write down the critical points of the nonlinear system of differential equations:
\begin{align*}
  \frac{dx}{dt} &= y(x^2 + 1) \\
  \frac{dy}{dt} &= 2xy^2.
\end{align*}

Let $B$ be the unit ball in the plane and let $u$ be a solution of the boundary value problem:
\begin{align*}
  \Delta u &= C \quad \text{in } B \\
  \frac{\partial u}{\partial n} &= 1 \quad \text{on } \partial B
\end{align*}
where $\Delta$ denotes the Laplace operator, $\partial B$ denotes the boundary of $B$ and $\frac{\partial u}{\partial n}$ denotes the outer normal derivative on the boundary. Evaluate $C$, given that it is a constant.

Write down the general solution of the linear system:
\begin{align*}
  \frac{dx}{dt} &= x + y \\
  \frac{dy}{dt} &= 4x - 2y.
\end{align*}

What is the smallest positive value of $\lambda$ such that the problem:
\begin{align*}
  u'' + \lambda u &= 0 \quad \text{in } ]0, 1[ \\
  u(0) = u(1) &\quad \text{and} \quad u'(0) = u'(1)
\end{align*}
has a solution such that $u \not\equiv 0$?

Let $f : \R \to \R$ be a continuous function. Write down the solution of the problem:
\begin{align*}
  \frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial t^2} - \frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial x^2} &= 0, \quad t > 0, \\
  u(x, 0) &= f(x), \quad x \in \R, \\
  \frac{\partial u}{\partial t}(x, 0) &= 0, \quad x \in \R.
\end{align*}

Find the general solution of the system:
\begin{align*}
  \frac{dx}{dt} &= x + y \\
  \frac{dy}{dt} &= 4x - 2y.
\end{align*}

Let $S_7$ denote the symmetric group of all permutations of the symbols $\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7\}$. Pick out the true statements:
\begin{enumerate}[a.]
  \item $S_7$ has an element of order $10$;
  \item $S_7$ has an element of order $15$;
  \item the order of any element of $S_7$ is at most $12$.
\end{enumerate}

Let $A \in \M_3(\R)$ which is \textbf{not} a diagonal matrix. Pick out the cases when $A$ is diagonalizable over $\R$:
\begin{enumerate}[a.]
  \item when $A^2 = A$;
  \item when $(A - 3I)^2 = 0$;
  \item when $A^2 + I = 0$.
\end{enumerate}

Let $A \in \M_3(\R)$ which is \textbf{not} a diagonal matrix. Let $p$ be a polynomial (in one variable), with real coefficients and of degree $3$ such that $p(A) = 0$. Pick out the true statements:
\begin{enumerate}[a.]
  \item $p = cp_A$ where $c \in \R$ and $p_A$ is the characteristic polynomial of $A$;
  \item if $p$ has a complex root (\emph{i.e.}\ a root with non-zero imaginary part), then $p = cp_A$, with $c$ and $p_A$ as above;
  \item if $p$ has a complex root, then $A$ is diagonalizable over $\C$.
\end{enumerate}

Let $f \in \mathcal{C}[-\pi, \pi]$. Evaluate:
\begin{enumerate}[a.]
  \item $\displaystyle\lim_{n \to \infty} \int_{-\pi}^{\pi} f(t)\cos nt\,dt$;
  \item $\displaystyle\lim_{n \to \infty} \int_{-\pi}^{\pi} f(t)\cos^2 nt\,dt$;
\end{enumerate}

In each of the following cases, examine whether the given sequence (or series) of functions converges uniformly over the given domain:
\begin{enumerate}[a.]
  \item $f_n(x) = \dfrac{nx}{1+nx}$, $x \in ]0, \infty[$;
  \item $\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{n\sin nx}{e^n}$, $x \in [0, \pi]$;
  \item $f_n(x) = \dfrac{x^n}{1+x^n}$, $x \in [0, 2]$.
\end{enumerate}

Let $A$ and $B$ be subsets of $\R^n$. Define $A + B = \{x + y \mid x \in A,\ y \in B\}$. Pick out the true statements:
\begin{enumerate}[a.]
  \item if $A$ and $B$ are closed sets, then $A + B$ is a closed set;
  \item if $A$ is an open set and if $B$ is a closed set, then $A + B$ is an open set;
  \item if $A$ and $B$ are compact sets, then so is $A + B$.
\end{enumerate}

Let $X$ and $Y$ be metric spaces and let $f : X \to Y$ be a mapping. Pick out the true statements:
\begin{enumerate}[a.]
  \item if $f$ is uniformly continuous, then the image of every Cauchy sequence in $X$ is a Cauchy sequence in $Y$;
  \item if $X$ is complete and if $f$ is continuous, then the image of every Cauchy sequence in $X$ is a Cauchy sequence in $Y$;
  \item if $Y$ is complete and if $f$ is continuous, then the image of every Cauchy sequence in $X$ is a Cauchy sequence in $Y$;
\end{enumerate}

Let $X_j = \mathcal{C}[0, 1]$ with the metric $d_1$ (see Question 3.7(b) above) when $j = 1$, the metric $d_2(f, g) = \left(\int_0^1 |f(t) - g(t)|^2\,dt\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}$ when $j = 2$ and the usual `sup-norm' metric when $j = 3$. Let $\mathrm{id} : \mathcal{C}[0, 1] \to \mathcal{C}[0, 1]$ be the identity map. Pick out the true statements:
\begin{enumerate}[a.]
  \item $\mathrm{id} : X_2 \to X_1$ is continuous;
  \item $\mathrm{id} : X_1 \to X_3$ is continuous;
  \item $\mathrm{id} : X_3 \to X_2$ is continuous.
\end{enumerate}

Let $a_i$, $1 \le i \le n$ be non-negative real numbers. Let $S$ denote their sum. Pick out the true statements:
\begin{enumerate}[a.]
  \item $\prod_{k=1}^{n} (1 + a_k) \ge 1 + S$;
  \item $\prod_{k=1}^{n} (1 + a_k) \le 1 + \frac{S}{1!} + \frac{S^2}{2!} + \dots + \frac{S^n}{n!}$;
  \item $\prod_{k=1}^{n} (1 + a_k) \le \frac{1}{1 - S}$, if $S < 1$.
\end{enumerate}

Let $G$ be the group of all invertible $2 \times 2$ upper triangular matrices (under matrix multiplication). Pick out the normal subgroups of $G$ from the following:
